---
description: Keep Arabic clause order when a desktop chat paragraph also contains English words.
alwaysApply: true
---

Arabic display: native
This is the Cursor desktop app. It draws Arabic itself. Write every reply in normal logical Arabic.
Do not run shape.mjs. Do not reverse letters. Do not paste presentation forms. Ignore a visual display line if one appears.
The desktop chat lays text out left to right. Each Latin word is a split, and the Arabic run before that word is drawn after it. A long paragraph breaks once per Latin word. Backticks, bold, and links are extra splits, because each formatted piece is drawn on its own and the outer marks cannot cross it.
Do this for every paragraph that contains both Arabic letters and Latin letters, however long it is and however many Latin words it has.
Put U+2067 at the first character of that paragraph and U+2069 at the last character. One pair covers the whole paragraph. Do not put U+2069 in the middle.
Write the words in normal logical order. Do not use U+202B or U+202C.
Keep every Latin word as plain text inside that same paragraph. Do not wrap a Latin word in backticks, bold, or a link.
A bullet or a numbered item is its own paragraph. Mark each mixed item. A reply with several mixed paragraphs needs one pair per paragraph, including the first.
Leave a paragraph that is only Arabic, or only English, unwrapped. An English-only code block stays its own block, unwrapped, and is not placed inside an Arabic paragraph.
Do not edit the existing arabic plugin.

Example, one long paragraph, several Latin words, plain text, one pair of marks around the whole paragraph:

عنوان الشات بقى BIM Work and Apps، والشغل هنا هيبقى خاص بـ BIM: النمذجة، التطبيقات، وسير العمل.

Surround that whole paragraph with U+2067 and U+2069.
